      "id": "l0s4s7x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning and Importance of DPSP"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Directive Principles of State Policy (DPSP) are enumerated in Part IV of the Constitution, spanning Articles 36 to 51.",
        "The idea of DPSPs was borrowed from the Irish Constitution of 1937, which had copied it from the Spanish Constitution.",
        "DPSPs are non-justiciable in nature, meaning they are not legally enforceable by the courts for their violation.",
        "The principles are broadly categorized into three groups: Socialist, Gandhian, and Liberal–Intellectual Principles.",
        "DPSPs aim to establish economic and social democracy and realize the ideal of a Welfare State in India."
      ],
      "id": "orhwc9j"
    },
